Write
\[
\Lambda_N=\sqrt{\log N\log\log N},\qquad L_N^c=\exp(c\Lambda_N).
\]
The two strongest findings are the recursive improvement and a prime-field DL transfer that avoids the usual NFS rank assumption.

**1. Recursive factoring: the fixed-point calculation survives scrutiny.**

The published comparison is Lenstra–Pomerance, “A rigorous time bound for factoring integers,” *JAMS* **5** (1992), 483–516, giving expected \(L_N[1/2,1]\) complete factorization. [Original paper](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf).

The source chain proves its moment estimates for arbitrary fixed \(a>0\), before the final choice \(a=\theta/\sqrt2\). That freedom is essential: keeping the old choice of \(a\) does not give the optimized recursion.

Suppose the shape test uses a factoring algorithm of constant \(C\). Since its two inputs are at most \(\sqrt{2N}\), its cost is
\[
L_N^{C/\sqrt2+o(1)}.
\]
The parent’s exponent is therefore
\[
\max\left\{
a+\frac C{\sqrt2},\
a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\
2a
\right\}.                                                    \tag{1}
\]
The batch-smoothness \(B\) term is already covered by \(2a\). This is the ledger in [Theorem A, Step 10](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmA_proof.md:309), with its shape-test entry replaced.

At a recursive fixed point, put
\[
a=\left(1-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)C.
\]
Equating the two relation terms gives
\[
C=a+\frac{\theta}{2a},
\qquad
\boxed{C_\theta^2=(1+\sqrt2)\theta}.
\]
Moreover,
\[
2a=(2-\sqrt2)C<C,
\]
so linear algebra does not obstruct this solution.

For an actual theorem, choose \(C>C_\theta\) and retain positive slack in every inequality. For the maximum expected cost on integers up to \(N\), the resulting recurrence has the form
\[
T(N)\le
L_N^{a+\varepsilon}\,T(\sqrt{2N})
+
L_N^{a+\theta/(2a)+\varepsilon}
+
L_N^{2a+\varepsilon},
\]
up to polynomial factors in \(\log N\). Strong induction closes it. Complete factorization adds only \(O(\log N)\) splitting calls. Recursive shape-test coins depend on \(u,v\), hence on \(R\), and preserve transcript invariance.

Some finite-parameter checks, before adding positive slack:

| \(d,k\) | Optimized \(\theta\) | Recursive threshold \(C_\theta\) |
|---|---:|---:|
| \(9,13\) | \(16/39\) | \(0.995212\) |
| \(9,1000\) | \(0.30085\) | \(0.852242\) |
| **\(15,1000\)** | **\(0.18840625\)** | **\(0.674428\)** |
| \(31,1000\) | \(0.094703125\) | \(0.478156\) |

Thus the \(1/\sqrt2\) floor is genuinely removed. The recursive formula was already anticipated in [hypD](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hypD_results.md:104); the later supply results make its use substantive.

Because
\[
\inf_{k,\eta_0}\theta=\frac3{d+1},
\]
the recursive constants tend to zero as fixed odd \(d\) increases. Consequently:

> **PROVED from the internal chain:** for every \(c>0\), some fixed-parameter Las Vegas algorithm completely factors every \(N\) in expected \(L_N[1/2,c]\) time.

There is also a single-algorithm consequence. Dovetail all validated fixed-parameter algorithms with fixed positive time shares. For each \(c>0\), one component supplies that bound; its fixed scheduling overhead does not affect the exponent. This yields
\[
\boxed{\mathbb E T(N)=\exp(o(\Lambda_N)).}
\]
This portfolio argument needs no uniform growing-degree estimate. It supplies **no specified rate** for the \(o(\Lambda_N)\), however, and therefore proves no \(L[\alpha]\) bound with fixed \(\alpha<1/2\).

**Missing lemma:** none beyond A’s internal ingredients; the remaining task is a formal recursive cost lemma.

**First step:** write that lemma using the maximum expected complete-factorization cost up to \(N\), positive exponent slack, and the existing absorbing-factor-exit bookkeeping. This should precede growing-degree work.

---

**2. Prime-field discrete logarithms: exact certificate dependencies give a stronger transfer than ordinary NFS linear algebra.**

The best unconditional prime-field bound I could verify remains
\[
L_p[1/2,\sqrt2].
\]
The exact reference is Pomerance, “Fast, rigorous factorization and discrete logarithm algorithms,” in *Discrete Algorithms and Complexity* (1987), 119–143. I found no published unconditional improvement of its \(\sqrt2\) constant for all prime fields. [Original paper](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/disclog.pdf).

Merely replacing NFS parity columns by columns modulo a large prime is insufficient. LV6 establishes squares, not arbitrary large-prime power relations. Nor does abundant relation supply establish the usual full-rank assertion.

There is a different route.

A descent certificate represents
\[
R_i=\operatorname{ev}(c_i),\qquad
c_i=(q_i,\gamma_i)\in\mathbb Q^\times\times K^\times.
\]
All certificates lie in one explicitly given rational algebra
\[
E=\mathbb Q\times K.
\]

**KNOWN:** Lenstra–Silverberg, *Foundations of Computational Mathematics* **18** (2018), 159–180, Theorem 1.11(i), computes generators for the exact multiplicative-relation lattice of supplied elements of a rational algebra in deterministic polynomial time. Its field case is Ge’s algorithm. It does not require computing the full class group or fundamental unit group. [Paper, Theorem 1.11 and §8](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.08843).

That theorem permits the following argument.

Take a primitive root \(z\bmod p\), and write the target as \(h=z^x\). Draw independent uniform
\[
a_i,b_i\pmod{p-1},\qquad R_i=z^{a_i}h^{b_i}.
\]
Pass only \(R_i\) to the descent procedure. Conditional on \(R_i\), the label \(b_i\) remains uniform.

The supply lemmas extend to this sampling law. For prime input \(p\), their coprimality and size hypotheses still hold. A uniform element of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\) is a square with probability \(1/2\), so the square-based split lower bound gives a lower bound for uniform elements with only that constant loss.

Now collect sufficiently many certificates. Let \(t=f_d\). The rational components are supported on primes at most \(B\) and primes dividing \(g_1\). The algebraic components are \(S\)-units supported above primes at most \(B\) and primes dividing \(t\). Thus their generated group has free rank at most
\[
r\le (d+1)\pi(B)+d\,\omega(t)+\omega(g_1)+r_1+r_2-1
   =B\,L_p^{o(1)}.                                           \tag{2}
\]
This is a rank bound, not a generation assumption. It requires neither factoring \(t\) nor computing the ambient \(S\)-unit group.

With \(m\ge r+3\) rows, use Lenstra–Silverberg to compute
\[
\mathcal L=\left\{e\in\mathbb Z^m:\prod_i c_i^{e_i}=1\right\}.
\]
Its rank is at least three.

For every prime \(\ell\mid p-1\), this lattice contains a vector nonzero modulo \(\ell\):

- For odd \(\ell\), its saturation quotient has no \(\ell\)-torsion.
- For \(\ell=2\), that quotient embeds in
  \(\mu(\mathbb Q\times K)\), which has order at most four because \(d\) is odd. A rank-three lattice contained in \(2\mathbb Z^m\) would force saturation index at least eight.

Consequently a computed generating set contains an eligible \(e\). Evaluating its exact dependency gives
\[
\sum_i e_i a_i+x\sum_i e_i b_i\equiv0\pmod{p-1}.              \tag{3}
\]
Conditional on the certificates and the chosen \(e\), the second coefficient is uniform modulo \(\ell\). It is invertible modulo \(\ell^v\) with probability \(1-1/\ell\). Solve (3) modulo each prime-power divisor of \(p-1\), then combine by CRT. Fresh rows handle unsuccessful coefficients; final exponentiation verifies the result.

Factor \(p-1\) first to certify a primitive root and obtain its prime powers. An arbitrary input base is handled by computing its ambient logarithm as well.

The important cost qualification is that exact integer dependencies are **not** known here to cost \(B^{2+o(1)}\). Let their polynomial cost be bounded by
\[
B^\kappa(\log p)^{O(1)}
\]
for some absolute constant \(\kappa\). The polynomial-time theorem is uniform in the algebra’s dimension, so \(\kappa\) need not grow with fixed \(d\).

If factoring has constant \(\tau\), the DL exponent is bounded by
\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{
\tau,\
a+\frac{\tau}{\sqrt2},\
a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\
\kappa a
\right\}.}                                                   \tag{4}
\]
This includes factoring \(p-1\), relation collection, batch smoothness, and exact post-processing.

For any requested \(c>0\), recursion supplies \(\tau<c\). Choose
\[
0<a<\min\{c-\tau/\sqrt2,\ c/\kappa\},
\qquad
0<\theta<2a(c-a).
\]
A sufficiently large **fixed** degree and then sufficiently large fixed \(k\) supply this \(\theta\). Every term in (4) is then below \(c\).

> **PROVED reduction from the internal supply chain:** prime-field DL admits every positive \(L_p[1/2,c]\) constant, and a portfolio gives expected \(\exp(o(\Lambda_p))\) time.

Even without device 7, Theorem A’s constants approaching \(1/\sqrt2\), substituted into (4), give DL constants arbitrarily close to \(1/\sqrt2\) from above.

**Missing lemma:** no additional analytic or rank conjecture. The prime-input transfer and exact-certificate reduction above need an independent audit and a careful implementation-level statement.

**First step:** formulate a certificate-dependency proposition for \(E=\mathbb Q\times K\), including (2), the torsion argument, and hidden-label independence. Do not claim the same finite-degree constant as factoring: \(\kappa a\) must be charged.

There is a useful additional consequence. For factoring, an exact dependency with an odd coefficient gives
\[
\left(\prod_i r_i^{e_i}\right)^2=1\pmod N.
\]
DD then makes its gcd fruitful. Thus exact dependencies offer an alternative to LV6, including its characters and algebraic square root. They may cost more, but could substantially simplify a proof whose objective is an arbitrarily small constant.

---

**3. Totients and multiplicative orders inherit the improvement immediately.**

**KNOWN:** factoring \(N\), followed by elementary arithmetic, computes
\[
\varphi(N)=\prod_{p^e\parallel N}p^{e-1}(p-1)
\]
and \(\lambda(N)\). For a unit \(a\bmod N\), factor an annihilating exponent and repeatedly remove prime divisors whenever the corresponding modular-power test succeeds. These are standard reductions; the published \(L[1/2,1]\) upper bound follows from LP92. [Shoup’s text, group algorithms and Euler’s function](https://shoup.net/ntb/ntb-b1.pdf).

For order computation, factoring \(N\) alone is not enough. Also factor the integers \(p-1\) for \(p\mid N\). There are only polynomially many such calls, each on an integer at most \(N\). Therefore
\[
T_{\varphi,\lambda,\mathrm{ord}}(N)
\le(\log N)^{O(1)}\max_{m\le N}T_{\mathrm{factor}}(m).
\]
No \(L\)-constant is lost.

**PROVED from A:** all three problems inherit A’s constants, the recursive constants, and the portfolio \(\exp(o(\Lambda_N))\) bound.

**Missing lemma:** none.

**First step:** state these as corollaries, with the additional \(p-1\) factorizations explicit.

Other immediate applications include modular square-root extraction and multiplicative-group structure. Likewise, for a point in a finite group whose cardinality \(H\) is already known, factoring \(H\) accelerates exact point-order computation. This applies to elliptic curves and Jacobians without implying a faster general discrete-log algorithm in those groups.

---

**4. Growing degree: recursion removes the shape-test barrier, but the one-level descent geometry still forces \(2/5\).**

Set
\[
t=\log N,\qquad \log B\asymp t^\alpha(\log t)^{1-\alpha},
\]
and let the largest initial split primes have
\[
\log B_1\asymp t^\beta(\log t)^{1-\beta}.
\]
Then
\[
k\asymp (t/\log t)^{1-\beta}.
\]

The initial split restriction costs an exponential on the scale
\[
k\log t\asymp t^{1-\beta}(\log t)^\beta.
\]
Thus one needs
\[
1-\beta\le\alpha.                                            \tag{5}
\]

Let \(d\asymp(t/\log t)^\delta\). The generalized base expansion and special-\(q\) box force the norm-size exponent to satisfy
\[
\sigma\ge\max\{1-\delta,\ \beta+\delta\}.
\]
Here the second term is the crucial \(d\log\ell\) contribution from
\[
V\gtrsim\sqrt\ell,\qquad F(a,b)\sim V^d.
\]
Smoothness testing at \(B=L[\alpha]\) then requires
\[
\sigma-\alpha\le\alpha.
\]
Optimizing \(\delta\) gives
\[
\frac{1+\beta}{2}\le2\alpha,\qquad \beta\le4\alpha-1.          \tag{6}
\]
Combining (5) and (6),
\[
\boxed{\alpha\ge2/5.}
\]

**PROVED feasibility obstruction within this one-level architecture:** this calculation survives removal of HECM. The HECM inequality in [lemmaM §2(c)](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lemmaM_results.md:132) is no longer the decisive restriction once shape tests recurse. The lattice-height/norm restriction remains.

At equality,
\[
\alpha=2/5,\qquad \beta=3/5,\qquad \delta=1/5.
\]

There are two quantitative issues.

First, the existing \(L^{o(1)}\) statements are insufficient when raised to \(2k\). A bound
\[
\gamma\ge(\log N)^{-C_d}
\]
can be disastrous if \(C_d\) grows rapidly. At the \(2/5\) schedule, one wants roughly
\[
\log(1/\gamma)=O(\log\log N),
\]
with controlled constants. That is compatible with polynomial dependence on \(d\), since \(\log d=O(\log\log N)\); it is incompatible with \(\gamma=\exp(-\Omega(d))\).

Second, the apparent \(2^{\omega(N)}\) obstruction can be removed in the relevant range by strengthening Theorem S′.

**PROVED elementary refinement: an index-free split bound.** Let \(G\) contain \(n\) primes, let \(S\) be a subset of its squarefree \(k\)-products with
\[
|S|\ge\binom nk/(2k),
\]
and suppose all its elements are at most \(X\), with \(2X^2<N\). Put \(h=2^{\omega(N)}\), and assume coprimality with \(N\). If
\[
n\ge8hk^4,
\]
then a uniform square \(R\bmod N\) satisfies
\[
\boxed{\Pr[R=u/v,\ u,v\in S,\ (u,v)=1]\ge\frac{|S|^2}{2\varphi(N)}.} \tag{7}
\]

Indeed, character positivity gives at least \(|S|^2/h\) square-ratio pairs. The number of pairs sharing a prime is at most
\[
n\binom{n-1}{k-1}^2
\le \frac{4k^4}{n}|S|^2.
\]
Discard them. The remaining pairs have distinct ratios modulo \(N\): equality modulo \(N\) implies equality over \(\mathbb Q\), and coprimality makes the representation unique. Dividing by \(\varphi(N)/h\) proves (7).

This condition holds at the proposed one-level scales after trial division to \(B\). At \(\beta=1-\alpha\), \(\log |G|\) exceeds the elementary upper bound for \(\log h\) by a factor of order \(\log\log N\). Charge every trial the full capped descent cost; then the old shape/success comparison introduces no replacement \(h\)-loss.

A conservative alternative to the ideal \(2/5\) schedule is worth investigating. Suppose the uniform moment argument yields
\[
\log(1/\gamma)=O(d^A\log t)
\]
for some fixed \(A\). The extra split cost requires
\[
1-\beta+A\delta\le\alpha.
\]
Together with the norm constraints, the formal optimum becomes
\[
\boxed{\alpha\ge\frac{A+2}{2A+5}.}                            \tag{8}
\]
Thus:

| Degree dependence of \(\log(1/\gamma)\) | Formal target |
|---|---:|
| \(O(\log t)\) | \(2/5\) |
| \(O(d\log t)\) | \(3/7\) |
| \(O(d^2\log t)\) | \(4/9\) |

These are **HEURISTIC parameter calculations**, not established algorithms. They explain why proving *some* exponent below \(1/2\) may be easier than obtaining \(2/5\).

**Exact missing lemma:** a uniform FM/LD* theorem for the chosen \(d(N),k(N)\), with explicit degree dependence, valid coefficient margins, controlled rejection probabilities, and the split acceptance cost from (7). A recursive \(L[\alpha]\) induction must then be proved directly; the fixed-degree portfolio is not a sufficiently fast black box.

**First step:** extract explicit dependence on \(d\) from Kern and FM, and bound \(\log(1/\gamma)\). Constants exponential in \(d\log d\) suggest starting with \(3/7\). The exact-dependency alternative above removes growing-degree Chebotarev and square-root costs from this audit.

To go below \(2/5\), one needs several descent levels or a different representation. The missing statement then concerns **whole descent trees**: prime factors produced by an accepted norm must enter the next field-dependent good sets with controlled total work. Marginal success for a uniform special-\(q\) prime does not establish this. Theorem K solves the freely sampled initial split problem; it does not automatically solve that endogenous-child problem.

---

**5. Extension fields: the polynomial-ring transplant is promising in a specific regime.**

The rigorous benchmarks must be separated.

- **KNOWN:** Bender–Pomerance, “Rigorous discrete logarithm computations in finite fields via smooth polynomials,” *AMS/IP Studies* **7** (1998), 221–232, gives the classical \(L_{p^n}[1/2,\sqrt2]\) result in its growing-extension range, including \(p\le n^{o(n)}\). [Original paper](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper115.pdf).
- **KNOWN:** Kleinjung–Wesolowski, *JAMS* **35** (2022), 581–624, proves expected time
  \[
  (pn)^{2\log_2 n+O(1)}.
  \]
  In fixed characteristic this is quasi-polynomial and already far faster than the proposed \(L[1/2]\) transfer. [Theorem and publication](https://doi.org/10.1090/jams/985).

Consider the intermediate regime \(p\approx\exp(n)\). The latter bound has logarithm \(O(n\log n)\), whereas the \(L_{p^n}[1/2]\) scale is \(n\sqrt{\log n}\). Improving the classical \(L[1/2]\) constant here would matter.

Represent the target field as
\[
\mathbb F_p[T]/(P(T)),\qquad \deg P=n,
\]
and construct over \(\mathbb F_p[T]\)
\[
F(g_0,g_1)=P(T)w(T).
\]
Use random coprime polynomial pairs \((g_0,g_1)\), an irreducible multiplier \(w\), and special-\(q\) lattices over \(\mathbb F_p[T]\).

Several components become easier:

- rational reconstruction and polynomial factorization have polynomial expected cost;
- there are no number-field Siegel zeros;
- divisor switching has a direct polynomial counterpart;
- exact function-field dependencies can be found from divisors, with a remaining constant-field calculation.

If the corresponding supply lemmas hold, the formal cost is
\[
\max\left\{a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\ \kappa a\right\},
\]
plus lower-order polynomial work and constant-field DL. As \(\theta\to0\) with fixed auxiliary degree, this again suggests arbitrarily small \(L[1/2]\) constants.

**SPECULATIVE**, because integer FM/LD* cannot simply be cited over a polynomial ring.

**Exact missing lemma:** polynomial-ring FM and LD* uniform in both \(p,n\), including smooth-polynomial densities, the restricted modulus family, irreducible candidate coordinates, and a geometric auxiliary function field. Its constants must remain valid in the chosen characteristic range.

**First step:** prove the analogue of Lemma W and the additive large-sieve rearrangement, then formulate the first moment. Start with \(\log p\asymp n\), where the parameter comparison is clean.

This does not settle fixed \(n\ge3\), \(p\to\infty\). A linear rational side only gives a degree-one residue-field map; those targets need a common irreducible factor of degree \(n\) and a higher-dimensional construction. I would not use Adleman–DeMarrais’s 1993 heuristic analysis as a rigorous baseline for that transfer.

---

**6. Fixed class groups and unit groups: reject the direct removal of GRH.**

Two literature corrections matter.

**KNOWN under GRH:** Hafner–McCurley’s original constant \(\sqrt2\) has been improved to
\[
3/\sqrt8=1.060660\ldots
\]
by Biasse–Erukulangara, “A proof of the conjectured run time of the Hafner–McCurley class group algorithm,” *Advances in Mathematics of Communications* **17** (2023), 1388–1405. Their theorem still assumes a generalized RH. [Paper](https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/amc.2021055).

**KNOWN under ERH:** de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski now prove subexponential class- and unit-group computation for arbitrary number fields. Their current Theorem 1.3 includes \(L_{|\Delta_K|}[1/2,O(1)]\) for fixed degree. Thus “general fields still require smooth-ideal heuristics under ERH” is outdated. [Current paper](https://koendeboer.com/publication/rigorousmethods/RigorousMethods.pdf).

Without GRH, the fully unconditional general benchmark I could verify for complete imaginary-quadratic class-number/group computation remains polynomial in \(D=|\Delta|\), on the \(D^{1/2+o(1)}\) scale. Louboutin gives the analytic class-number computation; enumeration of reduced forms also supplies a complete unconditional route. Faster customary Shanks computations must not be confused with an unconditional certification theorem. [Louboutin, *Math. Comp.* **71** (2002), 1735–1743](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-01-01367-9).

Why the transplant fails:

1. **The target field is fixed.** Replacing a discriminant by \(-Dw\) changes the class group. Factoring can choose an auxiliary field freely; computing \(\mathrm{Cl}(K)\) cannot.
2. **Existence is insufficient.** Second moments can produce relations without proving that the factor base generates the full class group or that the relation lattice is saturated.
3. **The size floor remains.** In the uniform-class model, representatives have norm about \(D^{1/2}\), giving the familiar ledger
   \[
   \max\left\{b+\frac1{4b},\,2b\right\}\ge1.
   \]
   Randomizing an auxiliary resultant does not remove that norm scale.
4. **Exact dependencies do not certify completeness.** Lenstra–Silverberg finds relations among supplied elements. It does not show that supplied units are fundamental or that supplied ideals generate every class.

For imaginary quadratic fields the unit group itself is finite and easy; the difficult target here is the class group.

**Exact missing lemma:** an unconditional, certified decomposition/sampling theorem for **every class of the fixed field**, together with generation and relation-lattice saturation at the desired subexponential cost.

**First step:** isolate the ERH uses in the new Arakelov-sampling theorem and test one proposed replacement there. A demonstration that only accelerates factoring norms has not removed the class-group bottleneck.

The significance would be enormous, but these devices currently supply no credible direct proof.

---

**7. Deterministic QS and coverage: existence over coins is not derandomization.**

The published worst-case benchmark is
\[
O\!\left(
\frac{N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N}{(\log\log N)^{3/5}}
\right)
\]
from Harvey–Hittmeir, “A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation.” Their 2026 large-order work improves an auxiliary subroutine, without establishing a smaller factoring exponent. [Factoring paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105), [2026 order paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11131).

The multiplier does not provide a short deterministic replacement preserving Lemma W. Pointwise domination over all residues modulo \(t\) requires support at least
\[
\varphi(t)/L^{o(1)},
\]
as [ldbound Proposition 7.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ldbound_results.md:610) shows. At fixed parameters that is polynomial in \(N\), far too expensive to enumerate. The analogous full enumeration of \((g_0,g_1)\) is also polynomial in \(N\).

This excludes preserving that particular domination argument with a tiny support. It does not exclude a different deterministic hitting-set proof.

For deterministic QS, the exact missing fruitfulness statement remains
\[
\#\{N\in S_n:H1\text{ holds and }\psi_N|_{\ker A_N}=1\}
=o(|S_n|).
\]
DD proves fruitfulness under a sampling symmetry. It does not establish this statement for the fixed near-\(\sqrt N\) QS root choices. H1 also remains unresolved at the requested subexponential scale. [detqs §6.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/detqs_results.md:399).

For coverage, a theorem with mean coverage \(\lambda=J^\vartheta\), \(\vartheta<1/4\), would give the internal exponent
\[
\frac{1+\vartheta}{6+\vartheta}<\frac15.
\]
For example \(\vartheta=1/5\) gives \(6/31\). But averaged multiplier success gives no such uniform covering theorem. [coverage38 §2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/coverage38_results.md:155).

**Exact missing lemma:** an explicitly constructible short seed list hitting a successful transcript for every input, or the original uniform coverage/fruitfulness theorem.

**First step:** choose one of those precise targets and prove a nontrivial deterministic hitting statement. Repeating the second-moment existence argument with “random” replaced by “enumerate” does not advance it.

---

**8. ECM families and Jacobians: retain the arithmetic-order corollaries, reject the general smooth-relation claims.**

For ECM, the canonical \(L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]\) small-factor analysis still depends on smooth-number supply in Hasse intervals. Barbulescu–Jouve obtain rigorous CM-family results conditional on Elliott–Halberstam for quadratic fields; the non-CM analogue remains hypothetical. [“ECM and the Elliott–Halberstam conjecture for quadratic fields,” *Acta Arithmetica* (2024)](https://www.math.u-bordeaux.fr/~fjouve001/ECM-EH.pdf).

The new devices do not immediately address this:

- multiplying a curve order by \(w\) does not produce another elliptic-curve order in the required Hasse interval;
- randomizing curve coefficients does not turn their traces into the linear lattice images used by Kern;
- batch smoothness recognizes smooth orders that are available; it does not make a short explicit curve family encounter them.

There is nevertheless an exact attractive target in the project. The hypothesis
\[
\Pr_{N\in S_n}[\text{first }j\text{ explicit curves all fail}]
\le j^{-1},\qquad j\le N^{1/14},
\]
at stage bound \(B=N^{1/8}\), would yield average \(N^{1/8+o(1)}\) factoring through its fallback theorem:
\[
\frac16-\frac7{12}\cdot\frac1{14}=\frac18.
\]
That is a useful feasibility calculation, but the required **HIT** theorem is essentially the unresolved ECM-family problem. [ecmavg](https://www.math.u-bordeaux.fr/~fjouve001/ECM-EH.pdf), [internal formulation](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ecmavg_results.md:210).

**Missing lemma:** this explicit-family hitting bound, retaining staged separation of the two factors.

**First step:** establish any power-saving zero-hit bound for a genuinely short explicit family. Covariance control for fixed-level divisibility alone is insufficient.

For Jacobian DL, the regime distinction is equally important. **KNOWN:** high-genus index calculus already has rigorous \(L_{q^g}[1/2,O(1)]\) analyses; Enge–Gaudry’s framework reaches the \(\sqrt2\) constant in its stated known-order/basis and genus-growth setting. [Enge–Gaudry, *Acta Arithmetica* **102** (2002), 83–103](https://doi.org/10.4064/aa102-1-6). Fixed-genus cryptographic Jacobians are a different asymptotic problem.

The prime-field proof above uses a certificate homomorphism from a subgroup of a rational algebra into \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). It supplies no corresponding representation for an arbitrary Jacobian. Changing a curve or a resultant does not preserve the specified target group.

**Missing lemma:** a target-preserving certificate construction with demonstrably smaller smoothness sizes and an efficient exact-dependency map into the given Jacobian.

**First step:** exhibit that homomorphism and its size ledger before attempting moment estimates. Until then, the defensible new Jacobian application is faster **point-order computation from a known cardinality**, inherited from factoring, rather than a new general DL bound.
