**Mechanism — SPECULATIVE.**  
Encode \(N\) by its base-\(p\) digit polynomial \(P_N(T)\), with \(P_N(p)=N\).  
Generate elimination identities in the small-characteristic algebra and its field components.  
Lift identities through truncated Witt vectors, recording the integer carry errors explicitly.  
Seek a small correction module whose dependencies become valid integer square congruences.

This reverses the direction of r19’s function-field transfer.

**KNOWN:** fixed-characteristic finite-field discrete logarithms admit quasi-polynomial expected time. Witt vectors provide characteristic-\(p^k\) lift structures. Neither theorem transfers those identities modulo an unrelated integer \(N\). [Kleinjung–Wesolowski](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.10668), [Hazewinkel](https://people.math.rochester.edu/faculty/doug/otherpapers/Hazewinkel-Witt.pdf).

**Payoff — HEURISTIC calculation.** Finite-field elimination would be inexpensive relative to \(L_N[1/4]\). If a corrected relation’s total carry norm satisfied
\[
\log X_{\rm carry}=O(\Lambda_{1/2}),
\]
then smoothing those corrections at \(B=L[1/4]\) costs \(L[1/4,O(1)]\). Polynomial powers of the resulting factor-base size preserve the exponent.

**Precise missing lemma — OPEN, highly implausible without additional structure.** Lift sufficiently many elimination identities to integer certificates with the displayed aggregate carry bound, at most \(L[1/4,O(1)]\) correction coordinates, and verified congruences modulo \(N\). Fruitfulness of the corrected dependencies needs its own argument or explicit heuristic.

**First step / kill test.** Lift one elimination step to precision \(p^k>N\). Track the multiplicative rank and total logarithmic norm of the correction terms after square cancellations. Kill the construction if ordinary size-\(N^{\Theta(1)}\) carry factors persist without an exploitable relation.

**Adversarial verdict — PROVED obstruction to the naive version.** A unital map
\[
\mathbb Z/p^k\mathbb Z\longrightarrow\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z
\]
requires \(N\mid p^k\). Thus a direct ring transplant is impossible for \((p,N)=1\). Only a new identity-with-corrections construction survives.

**Probability/significance:** **0.1%**. The payoff is enormous; the carry lemma is almost the entire breakthrough.

**7. Gassmann-equivalent fields as interchangeable certificate stores**

**Mechanism — SPECULATIVE proposal, then rejection.**  
Use nonisomorphic fields whose restriction-of-scalars tori are isomorphic or linked by an odd-degree isogeny.  
Transport multiplicative classes through monomial maps between conjugates.  
Choose a new field representation with refreshed coefficients.  
Hope that the selected evaluation modulo \(N\) survives up to a known square.

**KNOWN:** nonisomorphic fields with isomorphic multiplicative tori exist. [Prasad](https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.3173).

**Payoff if it worked.** Height-controlled universal transport would directly attack algebraic field-binding and make the nested \(L[1/3]\) size model relevant.

**Missing lemma — false in the following universal formulation.** Require an odd-degree torus isogeny whose pullback sends a selected embedding character to the old selected embedding character modulo twice the character lattice.

**PROVED kill.** Over a common Galois closure, the character lattices are permutation lattices. Odd isogeny degree makes the character map invertible modulo \(2\). If one embedding basis vector maps to one embedding basis vector modulo \(2\), Galois equivariance gives the same property throughout its orbit. Invertibility makes this a bijection of the embedding \(G\)-sets. The corresponding fields are therefore isomorphic.

**First kill test.** Reduce the monomial transport matrix modulo \(2\). Nonisomorphic examples necessarily mix embedding coordinates in the relevant sense.

**Verdict/probability/significance:** **0% for this universal formulation.** Restricted transport on selected certificate subgroups remains conceivable, but the isomorphic-tori theorem alone does not provide an escape. Idea 4 deliberately asks for a different, cohomological representation.

**8. Use coefficient digits as a limited-independence generator**

**Mechanism — SPECULATIVE proposal, then rejection of one proof strategy.**  
Split the random coefficients into digit blocks.  
Try to obtain high-order independence among candidate success indicators without increasing degree.  
Use limited-independence concentration to make every target descendable.  
Spend the field’s randomness on a strong zero-count tail instead of a second moment.

**KNOWN:** sufficiently high limited independence supports strong concentration bounds. The issue is obtaining that independence arithmetically. [Schmidt–Siegel–Srinivasan](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~srin/PDF/ch-bounds.pdf).

**Payoff — HEURISTIC calculation.** At \(1/3\), write \(E=\Lambda_{1/3}\), \(Q=\Lambda_{2/3}\), and \(d\asymp Q/E\). A mean candidate count \(\mu=e^{cE}\), together with suitable moments of order \(\asymp d\), could give a dead-target probability \(e^{-\Omega(dE)}=e^{-\Omega(Q)}\), enough for an all-target union bound.

**Precise missing lemma — OPEN generally; the triangular-fibre route fails.** Prove those high central moments while keeping coefficient height \(\log H=O(Q)\) and conditioning on local factors.

For \(r\) retained evaluation points, the generalized triangular kernel basis has leading multiplier \(\prod_i b_i\). The dimension-uniform fibre estimate used by this strategy needs roughly
\[
H\gg d\prod_i b_i.
\]
For \(r\asymp d\) and \(b_i\asymp e^{z/2}\), \(z\asymp Q\), this demands
\[
\log H=\Omega(dQ)=\Omega(\log N).
\]
That destroys the intended norm scale.

**First kill test.** Generalize Kern-U to three and then \(r\) evaluations, retaining its exact lattice factors. This already rejects the proposed uniform-fibre implementation; digit blocks do not remove those factors.

**Verdict/probability/significance:** **0% for this proof strategy.** High moments remain possible through a genuinely different arithmetic estimate. Limited independence cannot simply be declared from the number of coefficient bits.

**9. Spatially coupled relation graphs instead of individually successful trees**

**Mechanism — SPECULATIVE proposal, then rejection under diffuse labels.**  
Retain large-prime columns and organize relations in size layers.  
Seed the bottom layer with factor-base relations.  
Use spatial coupling and peeling, as in LDPC decoding, to propagate cancellation upward.  
Hope that global linear dependencies replace the requirement that every child have its own completed descent.

**KNOWN:** spatial coupling can improve decoding thresholds. Large-prime recombination is classical. Neither creates arithmetic coincidences among labels. [Kudekar–Richardson–Urbanke](https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1826), [Lenstra–Manasse](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1994-1250773-9).

**Payoff if it worked.** It would replace the completed-tree requirement by a global rank condition, potentially avoiding same-field overdetermination.

**Precise missing lemma — OPEN.** Construct relations whose large-child labels concentrate in reservoirs of effective size \(L[\alpha,O(1)]\), despite having norms up to \(L[\beta]\), \(\beta>\alpha\), without paying that concentration cost in lattice determinants or search probability.

**PROVED conditional kill calculation.** If \(M=L[\alpha,O(1)]\) relations each expose polynomially many child labels diffusely among \(V=L[\beta,\Theta(1)]\) possibilities, then
\[
\mathbb E[\text{accidental repeated labels}]
\ll \frac{M^2\operatorname{polylog}N}{V}=o(1).
\]
Reusing a forced target cancels that target but leaves fresh children. Peeling cannot eliminate terminal private columns merely by reorganizing the matrix.

**First kill test.** Contract forced-target edges and count repeated child labels and private terminal columns. Charge any CRT constraints used to force reservoir membership.

**Verdict/probability/significance:** **0% under diffuse-child statistics.** A successful arithmetic reservoir construction would be a different breakthrough; coding theory would then help exploit it.

**Adversarial ranking.** The survivors are **1 \(>\) 2 \(>\) 3 \(>\) 4 \(>\) 5 \(>\) 6**. I would start with the explicit two-level correlation system in **1**, and the constructive exceptional-set formulation in **2**. For **4–6**, require one exact transport or size identity before investing in asymptotic machinery. **7–9** should be rejected in the formulations analyzed above.
